{"major":{"id":"UCLA-MATHEMATICSECONOMICSBS","name":"Mathematics/Economics BS","kind":"major","catalog_year":"2026-27","source":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/major/2026/MATHEMATICSECONOMICSBS","sections":[{"name":"The Major","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"MATH 115A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 131A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 131B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 164","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 170E","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 170S","status":"inactive"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"MATH 174E","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ECON 141","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"STATS C183","status":"inactive"}],"count":1,"label":"Mathematics 174E or Economics 141 or Statistics C183","notes":"Select one course from:"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"MATH 134","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 135","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 136","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 171","status":"inactive"}],"count":1,"label":"Mathematics Elective","notes":"Select one course from:"}],"label":"Mathematics"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ECON 101","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ECON 102","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ECON 103","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ECON 103L","status":"inactive"},{"type":"text","text":"Select two courses from Economics 106E through 199B."}],"label":"Economics"}],"label":"The Major"}},{"name":"Honors Program","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"MATH 115AH","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 131AH","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 131BH","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Mathematics Honors Courses"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ECON 198A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ECON 198B","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Economics Honors Research","notes":"The thesis process requires enrollment in a two-term sequence for economics courses."}],"label":"Honors Program","notes":"To qualify for honors at graduation, students must (1) complete Mathematics 115AH, 131AH, and 131BH, (2) complete Economics 198A and 198B (the thesis process requires enrollment in a two-term sequence for economics courses), (3) present the thesis in Economics 198B, and (4) complete the major requirements with a minimum 3.5 grade-point average in both the upper-division economics and mathematics courses."}},{"name":"Computing Specialization","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"MATH 61","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 180","status":"inactive"}],"count":1,"label":"Mathematics 61 or 180","notes":"Select one course from:"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10B","status":"active"}],"label":"Program in Computing"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10C","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 15","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 16A","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 20A","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 40A","status":"active"}],"count":2,"label":"Program in Computing Electives","notes":"Select two courses from:"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"text","text":"Mathematics 149 through 159"}],"count":2,"label":"Mathematics Electives","notes":"Select two courses from:"}],"label":"Computing Specialization","notes":"Majors in Mathematics/Economics may select a Computing specialization by satisfying all the requirements for a bachelor’s degree in the major; and completing the following courses, with a minimum grade of C– in each course and a combined grade-point average of at least 2.0. Students must petition for admission to the program and are advised to do so after they complete Program in Computing 10B (petitions should be filed in the Mathematics Department Student Services Office). Students graduate with a bachelor’s degree in mathematics/economics and a specialization in Computing."}},{"name":"Preparation for the Major","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"MATH 31A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 31AL","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 31B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 32A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 32B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 33A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 33B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 61","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ECON 1","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ECON 2","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"ECON 11","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","status":"active"},{"type":"text","text":"Complete any one Writing II course."}],"label":"Preparation for the Major","notes":"Complete 11 courses, and one Writing II course. Select Mathematics 31A or 31AL."}}],"narrative":[],"overrides_applied":[]},"nodes":[{"id":"COM SCI 31","title":"Introduction to Computer Science I","units":"4","offered":["Fall"],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":false,"role":"prerequisite","groups":[],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, four hours; discussion, two hours; outside study, six hours. Introduction to computer science via theory, applications, and programming. Basic data types, operators and control structures. Input/output. Procedural and data abstraction. Introduction to object-oriented software development. Functions, recursion. Arrays, strings, pointers. Abstract data types, object-oriented programming. Examples and exercises from computer science theory and applications. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMSCI31"},{"id":"COM SCI 32","title":"Introduction to Computer Science II","units":"4","offered":["Fall"],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":false,"role":"prerequisite","groups":[],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 31","requires":{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, four hours; discussion, two hours; outside study, six hours. Object-oriented software development. Abstract data type definition and use. Overloading, inheritance, polymorphism. Object-oriented view of data structures: stacks, queues, lists. Algorithm analysis. Trees, graphs, and associated algorithms. Searching and sorting. Case studies and exercises from computer science applications. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMSCI32"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10A","title":"Introduction to Programming","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"required","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing","Preparation for the Major"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours; laboratory, eight hours. No prior programming experience assumed. Basic principles of programming, using C&plus;&plus;; algorithmic, procedural problem solving; program design and development; basic data types, control structures and functions; functional arrays and pointers; introduction to classes for programmer-defined data types. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10A"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10B","title":"Intermediate Programming","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"required","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 10A or Computer Science 31","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour; laboratory, eight hours. Object oriented programming in C++; operator overloading; memory management, copy and move constructors, copy and move assignment operators, destructors; iterators; data structures and their implementation, linked lists, binary search trees; inheritance and polymorphism; recursion, algorithms for sorting and searching. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10B"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10C","title":"Advanced Programming","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing Electives"],"column":2,"prereq_text":"course 10B","requires":{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10B","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours; laboratory, eight hours. More advanced algorithms and data structuring techniques; additional emphasis on algorithmic efficiency; advanced features of C&plus;&plus;, such as inheritance and virtual functions; graph algorithms. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10C"},{"id":"COMPTNG 15","title":"Introduction to Lisp and Symbolic Computation","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing Electives"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 10A","requires":{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours; laboratory, eight hours. Introduction to symbolic computation using Lisp programming language. Basics: list structures, recursion, function abstraction. Advanced topics: knowledge representation, higher-order functions, problem-solving algorithms and heuristics. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG15"},{"id":"COMPTNG 16A","title":"Python with Applications I","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing Electives"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 10A, Computer Science 31, or equivalent, with grades of C– or better","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":"C-"},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":"C-"},{"type":"condition","kind":"other","text":"equivalent"}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours. In-depth introduction to Python programming language for students who have already taken beginning programming course in strongly typed, compiled language (C++, C, or Fortran). Core Python language constructs, applications, text processing, data visualization, interaction with spreadsheets and SQL databases, and creation of graphical user interfaces. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG16A"},{"id":"COMPTNG 20A","title":"Principles of Java Language with Applications","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing Electives"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 10A or Computer Science 31","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours; laboratory, eight hours. Not open for credit to students with credit for course 3. Introduction to Java computer language. Class and interface hierarchies; graphics components and graphical user interfaces; streams; multithreading; event and exception handling. Issues in class design and design of interactive web pages. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG20A"},{"id":"COMPTNG 40A","title":"Introduction to Programming for Internet","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing Electives"],"column":2,"prereq_text":"course 10A or Computer Science 31 or equivalent, and one from course 10B, 16A, 20A, Computer Science 32, or equivalent, with grades of C– or better","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"and","items":[{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":"C-"},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":"C-"},{"type":"condition","kind":"other","text":"equivalent"}]},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10B","concurrent":false,"min_grade":"C-"}]},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 16A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":"C-"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 20A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":"C-"},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 32","concurrent":false,"min_grade":"C-"},{"type":"condition","kind":"other","text":"equivalent"}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours. Introduction to programming for World Wide Web for students with strong foundation in programming. HTML5 and CSS3 markup languages to design websites; client-side scripting with JavaScript to enable event-driven interactivity, animations, and cookie tracking; server-side scripting with PHP to render HTML pages, store, and retrieve data on server; and introduction to databases through SQLite3. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG40A"},{"id":"ECON 1","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"requir